WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.240
One of the biggest myths about the Israel-Palestine
conflict is that it's been going on for centuries,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.559
that this is all about ancient religious hatreds.

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:15.639
In fact, while religion is involved, the conflict
is mostly about two groups of people who claim

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:16.920
the same land.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.790
And it really only goes back about a century,
to the early 1900s.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:24.040
Around then, the region
along the eastern Mediterranean we now call

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.279
Israel-Palestine had been under Ottoman rule
for centuries.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:31.829
It was religiously diverse, including mostly
Muslims and Christians but also a small number

00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:33.239
of Jews, who lived generally in peace.

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:36.090
And it was changing in two important ways.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.960
First, more people in the region were developing
a sense of being not just ethnic Arabs

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.950
but Palestinians, a distinct
national identity.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:49.900
At the same time, not so far away in Europe,
more Jews were joining a movement called Zionism,

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.579
which said that Judaism was not just a religion
but a nationality, one that deserved a nation

00:00:54.579 --> 00:00:55.290
of its own.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.890
And after centuries of persecution, many believed
a Jewish state was their only way of

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:00.170
safety.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.790
And they saw their historic homeland in the Middle
East as their best hope for establishing it.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.500
In the first decades of the 20th century, tens of thousands of European Jews moved there.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.180
After World War One, the Ottoman Empire collapsed,
and the British and French Empires carved

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.880
up the Middle East, with the British taking
control of a region it called the British

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.150
Mandate for Palestine.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:26.370
At first, the British allowed Jewish immigration.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:32.470
But as more Jews arrived, settling into farming
communes, tension between Jews and Arabs grew.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.299
Both sides committed acts of violence.

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:40.000
And by the 1930s, the British began limiting Jewish
immigration.  In response,  Jewish militias

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.479
formed to fight both the local Arabs and to resist British rule.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:49.390
Then came the Holocaust, leading many more
Jews to flee Europe for British Palestine,

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.800
and galvanizing much of the world in support
of a Jewish state.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.300
In 1947, as sectarian violence between Arabs and Jews there grew,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:02.000
the United Nations approved a plan
to divide British Palestine into two separate states:

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.400
one for Jews, Israel, and one for
Arabs, Palestine.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.000
The city of Jerusalem, where Jews, Muslims, and Christians

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.549
all have have holy sites, it was to become a special international zone.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:12.989
 

00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:18.349
The plan was meant to give Jews a state, to
establish Palestinian independence, and to

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:21.900
end the sectarian violence that the British
could no longer control.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.200
The Jews accepted the plan and declared independence as Israel.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:32.379
But Arabs throughout the region saw the UN plan as just more European colonialism trying to steal their land.

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:37.540
Many of the Arab states, who had just recently
won independence themselves, declared war on Israel

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:42.700
in an effort to establish a unified Arab Palestine where all of British Palestine had been.

00:02:45.779 --> 00:02:50.969
The new state of Israel won the war. But in
the process, they pushed well past their borders

00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:55.739
under the UN plan, taking the western half
of Jerusalem and much of the land that was

00:02:55.739 --> 00:02:57.370
to have been part of Palestine.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:02.499
They also expelled huge numbers of Palestinians
from their homes, creating a massive refugee

00:03:02.499 --> 00:03:07.249
population whose descendants today number
about 7 million.

00:03:07.249 --> 00:03:12.370
At the end of the war, Israel controlled all
of the territory except for Gaza, which Egypt

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.919
controlled, and the West Bank, named because
it's west of the Jordan River, which Jordan

00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:17.999
controlled.

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:22.099
This was the beginning of the decades-long
Arab-Israeli conflict.

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:27.120
During this period, many Jews in Arab-majority
countries fled or were expelled, arriving

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.069
in Israel.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:33.819
Then something happened that transformed the
conflict. In 1967, Israel and the neighboring

00:03:33.819 --> 00:03:36.540
Arab states fought another war.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:43.859
When it ended, Israel had seized the Golan
Heights from Syria, the West Bank from Jordan,

00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:47.669
and both Gaza and the Sinai Peninsula from
Egypt.

00:03:47.669 --> 00:03:53.230
Israel was now occupying the Palestinian territories,
including all of Jerusalem and its holy sites.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:57.029
This left Israel responsible for governing
the Palestinians – a people it had fought

00:03:57.029 --> 00:03:58.499
for decades.

00:03:58.499 --> 00:04:05.650
In 1978 Israel and Egypt signed the US-brokered
Camp David Accords and shortly after that,

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.249
Israel gave Sanai back to Egypt as part of
a peace treaty.

00:04:08.249 --> 00:04:11.629
At the time this was hugely controversial
in the Arab world.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:16.100
Egypt President Anwar Sadat was assassinated
in part because of outrage against it.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:20.510
But it marked the beginning of the end of
the wider Arab-Israeli conflict.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.820
Over the next few decades, the other Arab
states gradually made peace with Israel, even

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.220
if they never signed formal peace treaties.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.380
But Israel's military was still occupying
the Palestinian territories of the West Bank

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:39.170
and Gaza, and this was when the conflict became
an Israeli-Palestinian struggle.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:44.350
The Palestinian Liberation Organization, which
had formed in the 1960s to seek a Palestinian

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:48.220
state, fought against Israel, including through
acts of terrorism.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.510
Initially, the PLO claimed all of what had
been British Palestine, meaning it wanted

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.340
to end the state of Israel entirely.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:01.350
Fighting between Israel and the PLO went on
for years, even including a 1982 Israeli invasion

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.400
of Lebanon to kick the group out of Beirut.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.800
The PLO later said it would accept dividing
the land between Israel and Palestine,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.460
but the conflict continued.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:19.580
As all of this was happening, something dramatic
was changing in the Israel-occupied Palestinian

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.590
territories: Israelis were moving in.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:26.860
These people are called settlers, and they
made their homes in the West Bank and Gaza

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.230
whether Palestinians wanted them or not.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:33.860
Some moved for religious reasons, some because
they want to claim the land for Israel, and

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:38.520
some just because housing is cheap — and
often subsidized by the Israeli government.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.780
Some settlements are cities with thousands
of people; others are small communities

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:43.300
deep into the West Bank

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:58.060
The settlers are followed by soldiers to guard
them, and the growing settlements force Palestinians

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:01.310
off of their land and divide communities.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:05.400
Short-term, they make the occupation much
more painful for Palestinians.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.670
Long-term, by dividing up Palestinian land,
they make it much more difficult

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.200
for the Palestinians to ever have an independent state.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.620
Today there are several hundred thousand settlers
in occupied territory even though the international

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.510
community considers them illegal.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:25.710
By the late 1980s, Palestinian frustration
exploded into the Intifada,  which is the the Arabic word

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.200
for uprising.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.250
It began with mostly protests and boycotts
but soon became violent, and Israel responded

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:34.400
with heavy force.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.600
A couple hundred Israelis and over a thousand
Palestinians died in the first Intifada.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:46.680
Around the same time, a group of Palestinians
in Gaza, who consider the PLO too secular and

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:53.230
too compromise-minded, created Hamas, a violent
extremist group dedicated to Israel's destruction.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:59.210
By the early 1990s, it's clear that Israelis
and Palestinians have to make peace, and leaders

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:02.500
from both sides sign the Oslo Accords.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:08.500
This is meant to be the big, first step toward
Israel maybe someday withdrawing from the Palestinian

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.800
territories, and allowing an independent Palestine.

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:18.280
The Oslo Accords establish the Palestinian
Authority, allowing Palestinians a little

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.990
bit of freedom to govern themselves in certain areas.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:27.480
Hard-liners on both sides opposed the Oslo
accords. Members of Hamas launch suicide bombings

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.139
to try to sabotage the process.

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:35.020
The Israeli right protests peace talks, with
ralliers calling Prime Minister Yitzhak Rabin

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.380
a traitor and a Nazi.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:42.090
Not long after Rabin signs the second round
of Oslo Accords, a far-right Israeli shoots

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:44.430
him to death in Tel Aviv.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:50.310
This violence showed how the extremists on
both sides can use violence to derail peace,

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:55.000
and keep a permanent conflict going as they
seek the other side's total destruction.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.000
That's a dynamic that's been around ever since.

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:02.680
Negotiations meant to hammer out the final
details on peace drag on for years, and a

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:05.540
big Camp David Summit in 2000 comes up empty.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.360
Palestinians come to believe that

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:13.720
peace isn't coming, and rise up in a Second
Intifada, this one much more violent than

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:14.400
the first.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.400
By the time it wound down a few years later,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:21.300
about 1,000
Israelis and 3,200 Palestinians had died.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:27.919
The Second Intifada really changes the conflict.
Israelis become much more skeptical that Palestinians

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:30.600
will ever accept peace, or that it's even worth trying.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:34.200
Israeli politics shift right, and the country
builds walls and checkpoints

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.709
to control Palestinians' movements.

00:08:36.709 --> 00:08:40.479
They're not really trying to solve the conflict
anymore, just manage it.

00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:45.600
The Palestinians are left feeling like negotiating
didn't work and violence didn't work, that

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.720
they're stuck under an ever-growing occupation
with no future as a people.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:55.879
That year, Israel withdraws from Gaza. Hamas
gains power but splits from the Palestinian

00:08:55.879 --> 00:09:00.509
Authority in a short civil war, dividing Gaza
from the West Bank.

00:09:00.509 --> 00:09:05.790
Israel puts Gaza under a suffocating blockade,
and unemployment rises to 40%.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.050
This is the state of the conflict as we know
it today.

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:11.709
It’s relatively new, and it’s
unbearable for Palestinians.

00:09:11.709 --> 00:09:16.339
In the West Bank, more and more settlements
are smothering Palestinians, who often respond

00:09:16.339 --> 00:09:20.559
with protests and sometimes with violence,
though most just want normal lives.

00:09:20.559 --> 00:09:24.100
In Gaza, Hamas and other violent groups have
periodic wars with Israel.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:30.990
The fighting overwhelmingly kills Palestinians,
including lots of civilians.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:35.959
In Israel itself, most people have become
apathetic, and for the most part the occupation

00:09:35.959 --> 00:09:40.779
keeps the conflict relatively removed from
their daily lives, with moments of brief but

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:42.089
horrible violence.

00:09:42.089 --> 00:09:43.600
There's little political will for peace.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.720
No one really knows where the conflict goes
from here.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:54.129
Maybe a Third Intifada. Maybe the Palestinian
Authority collapses.

00:09:54.129 --> 00:10:00.180
But everyone agrees that things, as they are now, can't 
last much longer -- that Israel’s occupation

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:04.759
of the Palestinians is too unstable to last, and that, unless something dramatic

00:10:04.759 --> 00:10:07.700
changes, whatever comes next will be much worse.

